# Erdős Problem #99 — Target B strengthened virtual noncrossing-area floor

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Run type:** exact cross-theorem strengthening  
**Target:** virtual-zonotope / support-depth interface  
**Depends on:** virtual-zonotope missing-crossing identity; Target B Section 44.1  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN

## 1. Correction to the first virtual-area estimate

The virtual-zonotope report proves the exact finite identity

`V_miss := sum_(tau<sigma, tau not cross sigma) |det(u_tau,u_sigma)|`

`= area(Z)-A_R`

and the exact lower bound

`V_miss >= H_partial^K/2`.                                      (1.1)

That report inserted the older Section-24 support-depth floor. In the pure rhombic-disk branch, Section 44.1 is substantially stronger and is already canonical.

Section 44.1 gives

`H_partial^K/D^2 = T/D - pi/2 + o(1)`.                          (1.2)

Using the established pure-disk train lower bound

`T/D >= 2.5395008183... + o(1)`,                               (1.3)

one obtains

`H_partial^K >= 0.9687044915... D^2 - o(D^2)`.                 (1.4)

Insert (1.4) into (1.1).

### Theorem 1 — strong virtual-area floor

Every sufficiently large triangle-free pure-rhombic global-minimizer survivor satisfies

`V_miss >= 0.4843522457... D^2 - o(D^2)`.                      (1.5)

Equivalently, retaining the symbolic dependence on `x=T/D`,

`V_miss/D^2 >= (x-pi/2)/2 - o(1)`.                             (1.6)

This is the correct strongest currently canonical support-depth consequence for the virtual noncrossing determinant mass.

## 2. Comparison with the old Section-24 insertion

The first virtual-zonotope report used

`H_partial^K >= (2sqrt(c)-sqrt(3))n-O(n^(2/3))`

and therefore displayed

`V_miss >= 0.1840497320... n-O(n^(2/3))`.

That bound remains true but is strictly weaker in the pure-disk branch. Since

`D^2/n -> c=2sqrt(3)/pi`,

(1.5) is equivalent to

`V_miss >= 0.534075... n-o(n)`,

with the decimal depending only on the already displayed Section-44 constant and `c`.

The exact symbolic form (1.6) should be preferred in further optimization.

## 3. Interface-population consequence

Let `I` be any subset of train tracks, `|I|=r`, and suppose every noncrossing pair carrying nonzero determinant mass has at least one endpoint in `I`. Since every determinant has magnitude at most one,

`V_miss <= #{unordered train pairs incident with I}`

`= r(T-r) + r(r-1)/2`

`<= r T`.                                                       (3.1)

Combining with (1.6) yields

### Corollary 2 — virtual area cannot be hidden in a sublinear interface

If all virtual determinant mass is incident with a train set `I`, then

`r/D >= [x-pi/2]/(2x) - o(1)`,                                 (3.2)

where `x=T/D`.

Using only `x>=2.5395008183...`, the right side at that endpoint is approximately

`0.1907`.

Thus any attempt to store all required virtual area solely in discarded/interface trains needs a **linear** train population. An `o(D)` interface is impossible.

More generally, if only a fraction `theta` of `V_miss` is carried by pairs incident with `I`, then

`r/D >= theta [x-pi/2]/(2x) - o(1)`.                            (3.3)

## 4. What this does and does not close

Theorem 50.1 controls the number of **deleted crossing edges**, not the number of deleted train vertices. Therefore Corollary 2 does not contradict the current dense-core pruning theorem: a linear population of low-degree trains can be removed while costing only `O(lambda D^2)` physical crossing edges.

The result does, however, eliminate a weaker escape that was still implicit in the first virtual-area handoff. The compulsory virtual determinant mass cannot be relegated to `o(D)` exceptional trains. Either:

1. a positive linear population of low-degree/interface trains persists; or
2. a positive fraction of `V_miss` is already internal to the retained coherent phase components.

Those are now the two quantitative branches of the global deficit-transfer problem.

## 5. Handoff

```text
LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.

Exact:
  V_miss >= H_partial^K/2.

Section 44.1 sharpens this in the pure disk to
  V_miss/D^2 >= (T/D-pi/2)/2-o(1)
               >=0.4843522457...-o(1).

Therefore a set of r trains incident with all virtual determinant mass must satisfy
  r/D >= (T/D-pi/2)/(2T/D)-o(1),
so r=Omega(D); at the current minimal T/D the coefficient is about 0.19.

Next split:
(A) Omega(D) low-degree/interface trains, or
(B) positive virtual-area mass inside retained coherent phase components.
Exploit each branch using exposed-hull terminal resources / local phase deficits.
```
